    stem:'A 35-year-old woman has had, for 6 months, frequent brief episodes of breathlessness, palpitations, sweating and a conviction that she is about to die, each lasting about 10 minutes. Cardiac, respiratory, thyroid and neurological investigations were all normal, and a diagnosis of panic disorder has been made.\n\nWhich of the following medications is the first choice in treatment?',
    options:['Antipsychotics','Mood stabilizers','SSRIs'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**SSRIs are the first-choice drug treatment for panic disorder**, and the course material says so directly. `L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology` lists the pharmacological treatment of anxiety and related disorders in explicit order: **“Psychopharmacology — ✓SSRIs; ✓Others … SNRIs, TCAs; ✓Benzodiazepines ‘Short duration, Not preferred’.”** `L17) Psychopharmacology` confirms it from the drug side, listing **“3) Anxiety disorders, obsessive compulsive disorder”** among antidepressant indications.\n\n*(This question is printed in the bank as the third of a linked chain, reading only “Which of the following medications are the first choice in treatment?” The case and its diagnosis have been restated here because the app shuffles questions and the original stem names no patient and no disorder. **Note also that the bank prints only three options for this question — a, b and c** — verified at 200 dpi; it is not a rendering cut.)*\n\n**What `L4,5` says about how to use them:**\n\n- **“2-6 weeks to start its effect”** — set the expectation at the first visit, or the patient stops the drug in week two believing it has failed.\n- **“One year then gradual withdrawal”** — treat well beyond symptom resolution; stopping early is the commonest cause of relapse.\n- **“Not addictive”** — the crucial contrast with benzodiazepines, and the reassurance most patients need before starting.\n- **“Not all patients respond from 1st trial”** — switching within or between classes is normal, not failure.\n\nA practical rule the decks do not state: in panic disorder the SSRI is **started at half the usual antidepressant dose** and titrated slowly, because early SSRI activation can transiently intensify anxiety and even trigger attacks *(not taken from the course material)*. This is why a short benzodiazepine bridge is often added — the strategy Q25 of this topic keys, pairing **paroxetine with alprazolam**, and which `L17` sanctions by listing **“1. Panic disorder”** among benzodiazepine indications under the heading **“(must NOT be used more than ONE month).”**\n\n**And drugs are not the whole treatment.** `L4,5` places **“Psychotherapy …. CBT”** *before* pharmacology, and `L19) Psychotherapy` records CBT’s use in **“anxiety, trauma related disorders.”** For panic specifically, CBT adds interoceptive exposure and correction of the catastrophic misinterpretation `L6) Myths about psychiatry` describes. The question asks which **medication** is first choice, so SSRIs is the answer — but the complete first-line answer is SSRI **plus** CBT.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Antipsychotics** | Panic disorder is not a psychotic illness. `L4,5` states of anxiety disorders **“Insight is intact”** and **“No psychotic features”**, and grades insight **“Intact …. (Anxiety)”** against **“Absent .... (Psychosis).”** `L17` confines antipsychotics to **“Schizophrenia, Schizoaffective disorder, Bipolar disorder, Psychotic depression, Treatment-resistant… major depression as an adjunct”** and tics/chorea. Using one here exposes the patient to extrapyramidal effects, akathisia — which would **worsen** subjective anxiety — hyperprolactinaemia and metabolic syndrome, for no benefit. Q23 of this topic tests the same two adverse effects |\n| **Mood stabilizers** | Belong to **bipolar disorder**. `L9,10) Mood disorders` builds its whole management around them — **“Initiate Mood Stabilizer,”** maintenance with **“Mood Stabilizer”** or combinations — and `L17` lists atypical antipsychotic and mood-stabiliser indications as bipolar, recurrent major depression, borderline personality and schizoaffective disorder. This patient has no manic or hypomanic history. They also carry real burdens: `L17` notes sodium valproate causes **“Stomach irritation, weight gain, tremors, menstrual irregularities and polycystic ovary,”** requires liver and CBC monitoring and **“is Contraindicated in pregnancy & lactation”**; lamotrigine requires monitoring for **Stevens–Johnson syndrome**; carbamazepine can lower the white cell count. Unjustifiable in a 35-year-old woman with panic disorder |\n\n**The classification point behind the question.** Q28 of this topic asks what group panic disorder belongs to, keyed *anxiety disorders*. This question is the consequence: **the class determines the drug.** File panic as a mood disorder and you reach for a mood stabiliser; file it as psychotic and you reach for an antipsychotic. Both errors are represented in this option list, and both are avoided by getting the classification right.\n\n**Bottom line:** panic disorder = SSRI, started low, continued a year, with CBT alongside and a benzodiazepine only as a short bridge. Not an antipsychotic, not a mood stabiliser.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'SSRIs are the first-choice medication for panic disorder — started low, continued about a year before gradual withdrawal, with CBT alongside; antipsychotics and mood stabilisers have no role.',
